<section class="cxx entity object">
<h1 id="Arcadia_Object">Arcadia_Object</h1>
<p>
<code>Arcadia_Object</code> is the ancestor type from which so called object types derive from.
</p>
<p>
<code>Arcadia_Object</code> types are allocated on the <em>heap</em> and can are referenced by <code>Arcadia_Object*</code> pointers.
</p>
<h2 id="Arcadia_Object_lifetime">Object Lifetime and Method Invocation</h2>
<p>
An ordinary method may be invoked on an <code>Arcadia_Object</code> or derived object only if the object
is fully constructed and has not entered finalization.
</p>
<p>
At the point of invocation, the receiver must be a GC root or transitively reachable from a GC root.
It must remain reachable throughout the invocation, including during garbage collections triggered
by nested calls. A C pointer, including <code>self</code>, does not by itself constitute a GC root.
</p>
<p>
The caller is responsible for satisfying these preconditions. An ordinary method is not required to
root its receiver automatically. Temporary roots can be established through the Arcadia value stack
or <code>Arcadia_Object_lock</code>; an owning object can retain references through its visit callback.
</p>
<p>
Constructors and finalizers are lifecycle operations rather than ordinary method invocations.
A finalizer must not assume that another unreachable managed object remains valid: the garbage
collector does not guarantee finalization order.
</p>
</section>

